        },
        "node_id": "MDc6UmVsZWFzZTk0NzY3NzU=",
        "tag_name": "v0.0.3",
        "target_commitish": "master",
        "name": "HashRing.add_nodes",
        "draft": false,
        "prerelease": false,
        "created_at": "2018-01-31T22:08:35Z",
        "published_at": "2018-01-31T22:09:39Z",
        "assets": [],
        "body": "Merge #2 \r\nRebuilding the ring each time the ring changes turns out to be a really slow operation. This hopes to address this by creating an `add_nodes` operation that can be performed on an empty ring that is faster than individually calling `add_node` for each node in the ring. So far, the benchmarks are promising, the `add_nodes` operation taking 2% the wall time of calling `add_node` individually for larger ring sizes. Right now, this operation will run in the event loop of our API servers, so making it go faster is a good way to reduce the impact of re-building the ring.\r\n\r\nHere is a result of the benches of adding nodes in the slow (add_node) vs fast (add_nodes) path, for 512 replicas per node:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5489149/35601571-a3f5e65c-05e8-11e8-8b31-caceb908f56d.png)\r\n\r\n\r\n - [x] add `HashRing.add_nodes(nodes)` method.\r\n - [x] add `HashRing.get_num_items()` method for debugging.\r\n - [x] add `HashRing.get_node(node_name)` method for debugging. \r\n - [x] add `hash_ring_sort` method that will sort the ring items array after we're done adding stuff to \r\nit.\r\n - [x] add `hash_ring_ensure_items_size` which will allow us to pre-allocate the `items` array, so we do not need to needlessly realloc it to grow, if we can compute the target size before hand.\r\n - [x] mod `hash_ring_add_node` to take a `doSort` param, to defer sorting of the items array. \r\n - [x] formalize benchmarks with pytest-bench\r\n - [x] test coverage around this."
      },
